<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (THE DATA PREPARATION PHASE INVOLVED SEVERAL CRITICAL STEPS TO ENSURE THAT THE DATASET WAS CLEAN, CONSISTENT, AND SUITABLE FOR MODELING FERTILIZER DOSAGE PREDICTIONS. EACH PREPROCESSING STEP WAS CAREFULLY DESIGNED TO IMPROVE THE QUALITY AND INTERPRETABILITY OF THE DATA, WHILE MINIMIZING BIASES AND INFORMATION LOSS.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1026 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (1. INITIAL EXPLORATION)
/E (1. INITIAL EXPLORATION)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1027 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (WE BEGAN BY ANALYZING THE DISTRIBUTION AND SKEWNESS OF THE TARGET VARIABLE, USAGE \(DOSAGE\), USING VISUAL INSPECTION TECHNIQUES. A HISTOGRAM COMBINED WITH A KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATE \(KDE\) PLOT WAS USED TO ASSESS THE OVERALL SHAPE OF THE DISTRIBUTION, AND THE NUMERICAL SKEWNESS COEFFICIENT WAS CALCULATED TO DETECT POTENTIAL ASYMMETRY.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1028 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (2. HANDLING MISSING VALUES)
/E (2. HANDLING MISSING VALUES)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1029 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (FOR SOIL NUTRIENT VARIABLES \(SOIL_N, SOIL_P, SOIL_K\), MISSING VALUES WERE IMPUTED BASED ON THE MEDIAN VALUE WITHIN EACH CORRESPONDING REGION. IF NO REGIONAL DATA WAS AVAILABLE FOR A GIVEN NUTRIENT, THE OVERALL DATASET MEDIAN WAS USED AS A FALLBACK. ROWS MISSING THE TARGET VARIABLE \(USAGE \(DOSAGE\)\) WERE REMOVED FROM THE DATASET TO AVOID INCONSISTENCIES DURING MODEL TRAINING.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1030 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (THE REMAINING MISSING VALUES ACROSS THE DATASET WERE THEN REVIEWED TO ENSURE MINIMAL DATA LOSS AND HIGH INTEGRITY.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1031 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (3. SKEWNESS REDUCTION)
/E (3. SKEWNESS REDUCTION)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1032 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (FEATURES EXHIBITING SIGNIFICANT SKEWNESS \(ABSOLUTE SKEWNESS GREATER THAN 1.5\) WERE IDENTIFIED AND SUBJECTED TO LOGARITHMIC TRANSFORMATION USING A LOG1P FUNCTION. THIS STEP AIMED TO NORMALIZE THE DISTRIBUTIONS AND IMPROVE MODEL PERFORMANCE, ESPECIALLY FOR LINEAR MODELS SENSITIVE TO FEATURE DISTRIBUTIONS.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
