1120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In this project, we designed an image classification model based on a transfer learning approach using MobileNetV2 as the backbone. First, we activated mixed precision training to accelerate computations on compatible GPUs. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (We initialized a MobileNetV2 model pre-trained on ImageNet, set its layers to non-trainable to preserve learned features, and built a custom Sequential model by adding a GlobalAveragePooling2D layer, followed by a Dense layer with 256 neurons \(ReLU activation\) and a Dropout layer to prevent overfitting. Finally, a Dense output layer with 38 neurons and softmax activation was added to predict the 38 plant disease classes.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The model was compiled with the Adam optimizer \(learning rate 0.0001\) and the categorical cross-entropy loss function, while tracking accuracy, precision, and recall as metrics. To improve model generalization, we used an ImageDataGenerator to apply various data augmentation techniques, such as random rotations, shifts, shearing, zooms, brightness changes, and flips. Images were resized to 128x128 pixels to balance training speed and performance.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (For training, we split the data into training and validation subsets \(80/20 split\) and generated batches with a size of 32. We also incorporated important callbacks such as EarlyStopping \(to halt training when no improvement is observed\) and ReduceLROnPlateau \(to reduce the learning rate on performance plateaus\), helping to optimize convergence. Overall, this pipeline enables efficient and robust model training for multi-class plant disease detection.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
