1824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In this project, we used transfer learning with the MobileNetV2 architecture, a lightweight and efficient convolutional neural network pre-trained on the ImageNet dataset. The base model was loaded without the top classification layer \(include_top=False\) and its weights were frozen to speed up the initial training process.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1825 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( We then built a new Sequential model by adding a Global Average Pooling layer, followed by a fully connected Dense layer with 256 units and ReLU activation, a Dropout layer with a 50% dropout rate to reduce overfitting, and a final Dense output layer with 38 units \(corresponding to the number of plant disease classes\) using softmax activation.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1826 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To optimize training on the GPU, mixed precision was enabled, and the batch size was increased to 64 to make better use of computational resources. The model was compiled using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001, categorical cross-entropy loss, and evaluation metrics including accuracy, precision, and recall.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1827 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Data augmentation was applied to the training set using a wide range of transformations, such as rotation, shifting, shearing, zooming, brightness adjustment, and both horizontal and vertical flipping.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1828 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( Images were resized to 128x128 pixels to balance model performance and training speed. The dataset was split into training and validation subsets \(80/20 split\) using the ImageDataGenerator class.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1829 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( Finally, callbacks like EarlyStopping and ReduceLROnPlateau were used to prevent overfitting and dynamically adjust the learning rate during training.)
/Pg 93 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
