2059 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The hyperparameters for the XGBoost model are carefully selected to optimize the model's performance. A variety of values for each parameter are tested during the hyperparameter tuning process. For example, n_estimators is tested at different values \(500, 800, 1000\), as this directly influences the number of trees the model will use. max_depth controls the maximum depth of each tree, while learning_rate adjusts the step size during gradient boosting. The subsample and colsample_bytree parameters control the fraction of training data and features to be used for building each tree, which can help reduce overfitting. Regularization terms \(reg_alpha and reg_lambda\) are also included to help prevent overfitting by penalizing complex models.)
/Pg 109 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2060 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (2-9. TRAINING AND FINE-TUNING PROCESS:)
/E (2-9. TRAINING AND FINE-TUNING PROCESS:)
/Pg 109 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2061 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The training and fine-tuning of the XGBoost model involves a randomized search approach, where multiple combinations of hyperparameters are tested using cross-validation. In this case, 70 different combinations of hyperparameters are explored, with 5-fold cross-validation for each combination to assess model performance. The randomized search uses neg_mean_squared_error as the scoring metric to identify the best model parameters that minimize the error. This process ensures that the model is not only well-fitted to the training data but also generalizes effectively to unseen data.)
/Pg 109 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
